The following features have been fully implemented, rigorously tested, and verified:

1. Two-pass single routine:
   - IMPLEMENTED AND VERIFIED.
   - `asm.cpp` uses a single unified function `assemblePass(const std::vector<ParsedLine>& lines, Pass pass, ...)` parameterized by `Pass::PASS_1` and `Pass::PASS_2`.
   - Pass 1 builds the symbol table, processes SET pseudo-ops, checks lexical/syntax errors, and computes instruction addresses without failing on undefined labels.
   - Pass 2 resolves label values, performs 24-bit operand range checks, generates 32-bit machine code, records unused label warnings, and detects undefined labels ("no such label").

2. Label errors:
   - IMPLEMENTED AND VERIFIED.
   - Detects and reports "bogus label name" for labels not starting with an alphabetic character or containing non-alphanumeric characters (e.g. `0badlabel:`, `_bad:`).
   - Detects and reports "duplicate label" when the same label name is defined more than once.
   - Detects and reports "no such label" (undefined label) in Pass 2.
   - Detects and warns about "unused label" without aborting assembly.
   - Verified in `tests/test02.asm` and logged in `tests/test02.log`.

3. Memory-dump listing & Advanced listing:
   - IMPLEMENTED AND VERIFIED.
   - Listing files (`.lst`) are generated using the preferred advanced format:
     `AAAAAAAA XXXXXXXX mnemonic operand`
     with label lines `AAAAAAAA label:` printed before instructions at that address.
     Branch instructions resolve and print target label names when defined, or raw numeric offsets.
   - Lines that fail to assemble keep their address slot but print `AAAAAAAA ERROR <message>` instead of a machine word (no fake zero word, which would read as `ldc 0`).
   - Verified against test01 expected values in `tests/test01.lst`.

4. Instruction table:
   - IMPLEMENTED AND VERIFIED.
   - Table-driven architecture using a single canonical table `INSTRUCTION_TABLE` containing mnemonic strings, opcodes (0-20, DATA, SET), and operand types (`NONE`, `VALUE`, `OFFSET`, `DATA`, `SET`).
   - Lookups are case-sensitive, requiring exact matches against `INSTRUCTION_TABLE` (e.g. `ldc`, `HALT`, `data`, `SET`); wrong case such as `halt`, `LDC`, `set`, `DATA` is rejected as "unknown mnemonic" (verified: MiniProject `test1`/`test5`/`test6` `halt` fails on both sample and our assemblers).
   - No scattered hard-coded instruction logic.

5. Test programs assemble:
   - IMPLEMENTED AND VERIFIED.
   - `test01.asm` (specification verification test) assembles cleanly and yields exact matching byte encoding.
   - `test02.asm` detects all required errors (bogus label name, unknown mnemonic, missing operand, unexpected operand, extra text at end of line, invalid numbers, operand out of range, undefined label, duplicate label) and emits no object file.
   - `test03.asm` (SET test) assembles cleanly and resolves forward and backward label references.
   - `test04.asm` (mul and div verification) assembles cleanly.
